<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Demaratus of Corinth</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Demaratus of Corinth was, according to Roman tradition, a nobleman of the Bacchiad family who fled Corinth in the 7th century BCE and settled at the Etruscan city of Tarquinii. There he married a local woman, and his son Lucumo moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and became its fifth king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-priscus">Tarquinius Priscus</a>. Ancient writers credited him with bringing Greek craftsmen and letters to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>.</p></div>
